@page "/"
@inject Lobby Lobby
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

@*
    The lobby: what is open, who is still waiting, and how to open another one.

    ⚠️ Static SSR, by default and deliberately (§3.11 C12). The table is the one interactive
    island in this client; a list of tables and two forms is a page, and a page whose every
    control is a real one — a <form>, a <button>, an <a> — needs no circuit to work at all
    (§3.11 A4). Sitting down is a GET, because it navigates; opening a table is a POST,
    because it makes one.

    🔥 Your name is typed here rather than configured, because a lobby is where a table learns
    who is coming. It travels in the URL. ⚠️ A name is not a credential and this page does not
    pretend it is one: whoever types a name that is already at a table takes that seat back,
    which is exactly what makes a browser refresh put you back in your own chair (§3.11 C16).
*@

<PageTitle>Burmese Poker — the tables</PageTitle>

<section class="lobby">
    <h1>The tables</h1>

    @if (_problem is { } wrong)
    {
        <p class="problem" role="alert">@wrong</p>
    }

    <ul class="tables">
        @foreach (var table in _tables)
        {
            <li @key="table.Id" class="open">
                <h2>@table.Title</h2>

                <p class="shape">
                    <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">🪑</span>
                    <span>@Shape(table)</span>
                </p>

                <p class="shape">
                    <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">🤖</span>
                    <span>@Opponents(table)</span>
                </p>

                <p class="state">@State(table)</p>

                @*
                    🔥 Shown even when every seat is held, and that is not an oversight. Blazor
                    keeps a dropped circuit alive for minutes before disposing it, so somebody
                    who reloaded finds their own seat still occupied — by their own ghost — and
                    a form that hid itself would lock them out of the table they are sitting at.
                    Sitting down under a name already here takes that seat back (§3.11 C16).
                *@
                <form class="sit" method="get" action="@($"/table/{table.Id}")">
                    <label for="@($"you-{table.Id}")">Your name</label>
                    <input id="@($"you-{table.Id}")" name="you" type="text"
                           maxlength="24" value="@Lobby.You" autocomplete="nickname" />
                    <button type="submit">Sit down</button>
                </form>

                <p class="watch">
                    <a href="@($"/table/{table.Id}")">Watch this table</a>
                </p>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>

    <details class="opening">
        <summary>Open another table</summary>

        @*
            🔥 No <AntiforgeryToken /> here, and that is the fix rather than the omission.
            EditForm renders one itself for a static SSR post; adding a second put two inputs
            of the same name in the form, the two arrived as one comma-joined value, and the
            post was rejected — with a page that had rendered perfectly. Found by pressing the
            button, which is the only thing that was ever going to find it (P13.3's rule: ask
            the server for what the page says it will do).
        *@
        <EditForm FormName="open" Model="Wanted!" OnValidSubmit="Open" method="post">
            <p>
                <label for="new-title">Called</label>
                <InputText id="new-title" @bind-Value="Wanted!.Title" maxlength="40" />
            </p>

            <p>
                <label for="new-seats">Seats</label>
                <InputNumber id="new-seats" @bind-Value="Wanted!.Seats"
                             min="@RoundEngine.MinimumPlayers" max="@RoundEngine.MaximumPlayers" />
            </p>

            <p>
                <label for="new-people">Of those, people</label>
                <InputNumber id="new-people" @bind-Value="Wanted!.People" min="0" max="@RoundEngine.MaximumPlayers" />
            </p>

            @*
                🔥 The difficulty dial, out of DifficultyLadder, strongest first (P19). P18 put
                the skill ladder here — which is a research instrument wearing a menu's clothes,
                because its rungs are far apart, cannot be asked for "a bit easier", and play a
                different and worse idea rather than the right idea badly. These are levels: the
                strongest rung there is, with a measured mistake rate (§3.12).

                ⚠️ Levels only. A menu with rungs in it as well would be the one thing §3.12
                forbids, and BotCatalog is still what the harness ranks.
            *@
            <p>
                <label for="new-difficulty">The computer plays</label>
                <InputSelect id="new-difficulty" @bind-Value="Wanted!.Difficulty">
                    @foreach (var level in DifficultyLadder.ByStrength)
                    {
                        <option @key="level.Name" value="@level.Name">@level.Name — @level.Description</option>
                    }
                </InputSelect>
            </p>

            @*
                🔥 Difficulty is per seat, and this is how a person asks for that (P19). A table
                of four identical bots is the least interesting table in the game. A checkbox
                rather than one <select> per seat, because the seat count is chosen in this same
                form and a static SSR page cannot grow controls as you type (§3.11 C12).
            *@
            <p class="mixed">
                <InputCheckbox id="new-mixed" @bind-Value="Wanted!.Mixed" />
                <label for="new-mixed">A mixed table — a different level in each computer seat</label>
            </p>

            <p class="note">
                The rest are played by the computer, and each is named for how it plays. A table
                with no people at it still deals, and is worth watching. A seat the computer
                takes over for somebody who has stopped answering plays as well as it can,
                whatever this says.
            </p>

            <p>
                <button type="submit">Open it</button>
            </p>
        </EditForm>
    </details>
</section>

@code {
    private IReadOnlyList<HostedTable> _tables = [];
    private string? _problem;

    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>Bound from the form, so it survives the post</b> — a static SSR page is built again
    /// from scratch on the way back, and a model held only in a field would come back empty.
    /// </remarks>
    [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "open")]
    private NewTable? Wanted { get; set; }

    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>The model is filled in here and never by an initialiser</b> (BL0008): a form post
    /// binds this property, and a property with an initialiser can be overwritten with null on
    /// the way through.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Wanted ??= new NewTable
        {
            Seats = Lobby.Opening.Seats,
            People = Lobby.Opening.People,
            Difficulty = Lobby.Opening.Difficulty,
            Mixed = Lobby.Opening.Difficulties is { Count: > 0 }
        };

        _tables = Lobby.Tables;
    }

    private void Open()
    {
        var wanted = Wanted ?? new NewTable();
        var seats = Math.Clamp(wanted.Seats, RoundEngine.MinimumPlayers, RoundEngine.MaximumPlayers);
        var people = Math.Clamp(wanted.People, 0, seats);

        try
        {
            var table = Lobby.Open(Lobby.Opening with
            {
                Title = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(wanted.Title) ? "A table" : wanted.Title.Trim(),
                Seats = seats,
                People = people,
                // A name off a form is a name off a wire: resolved, never trusted (P18).
                Difficulty = (DifficultyLadder.Find(wanted.Difficulty) ?? DifficultyLadder.Default).Name,
                // ⚠️ The spread is strongest first and cycles, so it fits whatever the table's
                // shape turns out to be — HostedTable takes as many of it as it has computer
                // seats (P19).
                Difficulties = wanted.Mixed
                    ? [.. DifficultyLadder.Spread(seats).Select(level => level.Name)]
                    : null,
                Seed = Lobby.NextSeed()
            });

            Navigation.NavigateTo($"/table/{table.Id}");
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException full)
        {
            _problem = full.Message;
            _tables = Lobby.Tables;
        }
    }

    private static string Shape(HostedTable table) =>
        $"{table.Seats} seats, {table.Plan.People} for people";

    /// <summary>
    /// How hard the computer is here — one word, or the mix itself.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>A mixed table has no one answer</b> (P19), so it is not given one: the levels are
    /// listed in the order the seats are dealt them, which is what the seat names on the table
    /// itself will say.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Opponents(HostedTable table) => table.Difficulties switch
    {
        { Count: 0 } => "Every seat is for a person",
        { } levels when table.IsMixed =>
            "The computer plays " + string.Join(", ", levels.Select(level => level.Name)),
        { } levels => $"The computer plays {levels[0].Name}"
    };

    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>What the table is doing, and why.</b> A table waits for the seats it was opened with —
    /// P13.4's finding, that an unanswered seat spends its whole patience on every question, is
    /// only fixable by something that knows whether anybody is there (BUILD-PLAN P13.6).
    /// </remarks>
    private static string State(HostedTable table) => table switch
    {
        { WaitingFor.Count: > 0 } waiting =>
            $"Waiting for {waiting.WaitingFor.Count} more.",
        { IsDealing: true } dealing when dealing.Board.Round > 0 =>
            $"Playing round {dealing.Board.Round}. Every seat is taken; your own name takes yours back.",
        { IsDealing: true } => "Dealing.",
        _ => "Nobody here. It deals again when somebody arrives."
    };

    /// <summary>A table somebody is asking for, as the form has it.</summary>
    public sealed class NewTable
    {
        public string Title { get; set; } = "A table";

        public int Seats { get; set; } = RoundEngine.MinimumPlayers;

        public int People { get; set; } = 1;

        /// <summary>Which level of <c>DifficultyLadder</c> the computer's seats play.</summary>
        public string Difficulty { get; set; } = DifficultyLadder.Default.Name;

        /// <summary>Whether to spread the dial across the computer's seats instead (P19).</summary>
        public bool Mixed { get; set; }
    }
}
